export const INTRO_PHYS_SCI_QUIZZES = [
  {
    id:`cp101`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Intro to Atoms & the Periodic Table`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`What is the smallest particle of an element that still has the properties of that element?`, ch:[`Molecule`,`Atom`,`Proton`,`Compound`], ans:1, exp:`An atom is the basic building block of matter and the smallest unit of an element that keeps that element's chemical properties. Molecules and compounds are made of atoms bonded together.` },
      { q:`Which subatomic particle has a positive charge?`, ch:[`Electron`,`Neutron`,`Proton`,`Photon`], ans:2, exp:`Protons carry a positive charge and are found in the nucleus. Electrons are negatively charged and neutrons have no charge.` },
      { q:`Which subatomic particle has no charge (is neutral)?`, ch:[`Proton`,`Electron`,`Neutron`,`Ion`], ans:2, exp:`Neutrons have no electric charge. Along with protons, they make up the nucleus and add mass to the atom without adding charge.` },
      { q:`Where are protons and neutrons located in an atom?`, ch:[`In the electron cloud`,`In the nucleus`,`Outside the atom`,`In orbitals only`], ans:1, exp:`Protons and neutrons are packed tightly together in the nucleus at the center of the atom. Electrons move around the nucleus in the surrounding space.` },
      { q:`The atomic number of an element tells you the number of:`, ch:[`Neutrons`,`Protons`,`Total particles`,`Electrons only in the outer shell`], ans:1, exp:`The atomic number equals the number of protons in an atom's nucleus. This number is unique to each element and defines what element it is.` },
      { q:`Elements in the periodic table are arranged in order of increasing:`, ch:[`Atomic mass only`,`Atomic number`,`Number of neutrons`,`Melting point`], ans:1, exp:`The modern periodic table lists elements from left to right and top to bottom in order of increasing atomic number (number of protons).` },
      { q:`Elements in the same column (group) of the periodic table tend to have similar:`, ch:[`Number of neutrons`,`Atomic mass`,`Chemical properties`,`Number of protons`], ans:2, exp:`Elements in the same group have the same number of valence (outer shell) electrons, which gives them similar chemical behavior, even though their atomic mass and proton count differ.` },
      { q:`Which of these is a noble gas, known for being unreactive?`, ch:[`Chlorine`,`Neon`,`Sodium`,`Oxygen`], ans:1, exp:`Neon is a noble gas in the far right column of the periodic table. Noble gases have a full outer electron shell, making them very stable and unreactive.` },
      { q:`An atom that has gained or lost electrons, giving it a net charge, is called:`, ch:[`Isotope`,`Molecule`,`Ion`,`Neutron`], ans:2, exp:`An ion is an atom (or group of atoms) with a positive or negative charge because it has lost or gained electrons. An isotope, by contrast, differs in neutron number, not charge.` },
      { q:`Isotopes of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of:`, ch:[`Electrons`,`Neutrons`,`Protons`,`Ions`], ans:1, exp:`Isotopes are versions of the same element (same number of protons) that have different numbers of neutrons, giving them different atomic masses.` },
      { q:`Which part of the atom determines most of its chemical behavior?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Neutrons`,`Protons in the nucleus`,`Electrons, especially outer/valence electrons`], ans:3, exp:`Chemical bonding and reactivity depend mainly on an atom's outermost (valence) electrons, since these are the electrons involved in forming or breaking bonds with other atoms.` },
      { q:`Metals are generally found on which side of the periodic table?`, ch:[`Right side`,`Left side and middle`,`Top only`,`Bottom only`], ans:1, exp:`Most elements on the left side and in the middle (the transition metals) of the periodic table are metals. Nonmetals are mostly found on the upper right, with metalloids along the zig-zag border.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp102`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Chemical Bonding Basics: Ionic vs Covalent`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`An ionic bond forms when:`, ch:[`Two atoms share electrons equally`,`One atom transfers electrons to another`,`Atoms share protons`,`Neutrons are exchanged`], ans:1, exp:`In an ionic bond, one atom (usually a metal) gives up one or more electrons to another atom (usually a nonmetal), creating oppositely charged ions that attract each other.` },
      { q:`A covalent bond forms when:`, ch:[`Atoms transfer electrons completely`,`Atoms share pairs of electrons`,`Atoms repel each other`,`Neutrons are shared`], ans:1, exp:`In a covalent bond, two atoms (typically both nonmetals) share one or more pairs of electrons, allowing both atoms to have a more stable outer electron shell.` },
      { q:`Ionic bonds typically form between:`, ch:[`Two nonmetals`,`A metal and a nonmetal`,`Two metals`,`Two noble gases`], ans:1, exp:`Metals tend to lose electrons easily and nonmetals tend to gain them, so a metal transferring electrons to a nonmetal produces the oppositely charged ions of an ionic bond.` },
      { q:`Covalent bonds typically form between:`, ch:[`Two metals`,`A metal and a nonmetal`,`Two nonmetals`,`Two ions`], ans:2, exp:`Two nonmetal atoms both tend to attract electrons, so instead of one giving electrons away, they share electrons to fill their outer shells, forming a covalent bond.` },
      { q:`Table salt (NaCl) is held together by what type of bond?`, ch:[`Covalent`,`Ionic`,`Metallic`,`No bond, it's a mixture`], ans:1, exp:`Sodium (a metal) transfers an electron to chlorine (a nonmetal), forming Na+ and Cl- ions that attract each other in an ionic bond, creating a crystal lattice.` },
      { q:`Water (H2O) molecules are held together internally by what type of bond?`, ch:[`Ionic`,`Covalent`,`Metallic`,`Hydrogen bond`], ans:1, exp:`Within a single water molecule, oxygen and hydrogen atoms share electron pairs, forming covalent bonds. (Hydrogen bonds are weaker attractions that occur between separate water molecules, not within one.)` },
      { q:`When an atom loses an electron, it becomes:`, ch:[`A negative ion (anion)`,`A positive ion (cation)`,`Neutral`,`A different element`], ans:1, exp:`Losing a negatively charged electron leaves the atom with more protons than electrons, giving it an overall positive charge and making it a cation.` },
      { q:`When an atom gains an electron, it becomes:`, ch:[`A positive ion (cation)`,`A negative ion (anion)`,`Neutral`,`Radioactive`], ans:1, exp:`Gaining an extra electron gives the atom more electrons than protons, so it becomes negatively charged, forming an anion.` },
      { q:`In a covalent bond, the shared electrons are most attracted to the atom with:`, ch:[`More neutrons`,`Lower atomic mass`,`Higher electronegativity`,`Fewer protons`], ans:2, exp:`Electronegativity measures how strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. The atom with higher electronegativity pulls the shared electron pair closer to itself, which can make the bond polar.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is most likely to form an ionic compound?`, ch:[`Carbon and hydrogen`,`Sodium and chlorine`,`Oxygen and oxygen`,`Nitrogen and hydrogen`], ans:1, exp:`Sodium is a metal and chlorine is a nonmetal, so sodium transfers an electron to chlorine, forming the ionic compound NaCl. The other pairs listed involve nonmetals bonding covalently.` },
      { q:`Ionic compounds tend to have:`, ch:[`Low melting points`,`High melting points and form crystal structures`,`No definite structure`,`Always be liquids at room temp`], ans:1, exp:`The strong attraction between positive and negative ions arranged in a repeating crystal lattice requires a lot of energy to break apart, giving ionic compounds high melting and boiling points.` },
      { q:`A chemical bond is best described as:`, ch:[`A physical mixture of atoms`,`The force that holds atoms together in a compound`,`A type of radioactive decay`,`The space between atoms`], ans:1, exp:`A chemical bond is the attractive force, resulting from the sharing or transfer of electrons, that holds two or more atoms together in a molecule or compound.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp103`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Balancing Chemical Equations`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`In a balanced chemical equation, what must be equal on both sides?`, ch:[`Number of molecules`,`Number of atoms of each element`,`Volume of gases`,`Number of reactions`], ans:1, exp:`A balanced equation has the same number of atoms of each element on the reactant side and the product side, even though the number of molecules can differ.` },
      { q:`Balancing a chemical equation upholds which law?`, ch:[`Law of Definite Proportions`,`Law of Conservation of Mass`,`Newton's Third Law`,`Ideal Gas Law`], ans:1, exp:`The Law of Conservation of Mass states that matter cannot be created or destroyed in a chemical reaction, so atoms present in the reactants must all appear in the products.` },
      { q:`In the equation 2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O, how many total hydrogen atoms are on the reactant side?`, ch:[`2`,`3`,`4`,`6`], ans:2, exp:`2H2 means two molecules of H2, each with 2 hydrogen atoms, giving 2 x 2 = 4 hydrogen atoms total on the reactant side.` },
      { q:`Balance this equation: __N2 + __H2 -> __NH3. What coefficients balance it (in order N2, H2, NH3)?`, ch:[`1, 3, 2`,`2, 3, 2`,`1, 2, 3`,`2, 2, 2`], ans:0, exp:`N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3 balances nitrogen (2 = 2) and hydrogen (3 x 2 = 6 on the left, 2 x 3 = 6 on the right).` },
      { q:`When balancing equations, you may only change:`, ch:[`Subscripts in formulas`,`Coefficients in front of formulas`,`The chemical formulas themselves`,`The state symbols only`], ans:1, exp:`Only the coefficients (the numbers placed in front of each formula) can be changed when balancing. Changing subscripts would change the identity of the substance itself.` },
      { q:`Balance: __Fe + __O2 -> __Fe2O3. Which coefficients work (Fe, O2, Fe2O3)?`, ch:[`4, 3, 2`,`2, 3, 1`,`4, 2, 3`,`2, 2, 2`], ans:0, exp:`4Fe + 3O2 -> 2Fe2O3 balances iron (4 = 2 x 2) and oxygen (3 x 2 = 6, and 2 x 3 = 6).` },
      { q:`In the balanced equation CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O, what does the coefficient "2" in front of O2 represent?`, ch:[`2 molecules of oxygen gas react`,`2 grams of oxygen`,`2 atoms of oxygen only`,`The reaction occurs twice`], ans:0, exp:`A coefficient in front of a formula tells you the number of molecules (or moles) of that substance involved in the reaction, so "2O2" means two molecules of O2.` },
      { q:`Which of these equations is already balanced?`, ch:[`H2 + O2 -> H2O`,`Mg + O2 -> MgO`,`2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO`,`Mg + O2 -> Mg2O2`], ans:2, exp:`2Mg + O2 -> 2MgO has 2 Mg atoms and 2 O atoms on each side, so it is correctly balanced. The others have mismatched atom counts.` },
      { q:`A skeleton (unbalanced) equation shows:`, ch:[`The correct ratio of reactants and products`,`Only the reactant and product formulas without correct ratios`,`The states of matter`,`The energy released`], ans:1, exp:`A skeleton equation lists the correct chemical formulas for reactants and products but has not yet been balanced with the correct coefficients.` },
      { q:`Balance: __Al + __HCl -> __AlCl3 + __H2. Correct coefficients (Al, HCl, AlCl3, H2)?`, ch:[`2, 6, 2, 3`,`1, 3, 1, 3`,`2, 3, 2, 3`,`1, 6, 1, 3`], ans:0, exp:`2Al + 6HCl -> 2AlCl3 + 3H2 balances aluminum (2 = 2), chlorine (6 = 2 x 3), and hydrogen (6 = 3 x 2).` },
      { q:`Why must chemical equations be balanced?`, ch:[`To make them look neater`,`Because atoms are neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction`,`Because reactions always produce gas`,`To increase reaction speed`], ans:1, exp:`Balancing reflects the fact that atoms are conserved during a chemical reaction; they are only rearranged into new substances, not created or destroyed.` },
      { q:`In the balanced equation 2H2O2 -> 2H2O + O2, hydrogen peroxide decomposes. How many oxygen atoms are on the product side?`, ch:[`2`,`3`,`4`,`6`], ans:2, exp:`2H2O contributes 2 oxygen atoms and O2 contributes 2 oxygen atoms, for a total of 4 oxygen atoms on the product side, matching the 4 oxygen atoms in 2H2O2 on the reactant side.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp104`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`States of Matter & Phase Changes`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`Which state of matter has a definite shape and definite volume?`, ch:[`Gas`,`Liquid`,`Solid`,`Plasma`], ans:2, exp:`A solid holds both its shape and its volume because its particles are tightly packed and locked in fixed positions, only vibrating in place.` },
      { q:`Which state of matter has a definite volume but takes the shape of its container?`, ch:[`Solid`,`Liquid`,`Gas`,`Plasma`], ans:1, exp:`A liquid's particles are close together but can move past one another, so it keeps a fixed volume while flowing to fill the shape of its container.` },
      { q:`Which state of matter has neither definite shape nor definite volume?`, ch:[`Solid`,`Liquid`,`Gas`,`Crystal`], ans:2, exp:`Gas particles move freely and are far apart, so a gas expands to fill both the shape and volume of whatever container holds it.` },
      { q:`The process of a solid changing directly into a gas without becoming a liquid is called:`, ch:[`Evaporation`,`Condensation`,`Sublimation`,`Deposition`], ans:2, exp:`Sublimation is the direct change from solid to gas, skipping the liquid state. Dry ice (solid CO2) sublimating into CO2 gas is a common example.` },
      { q:`The process of a gas changing directly into a solid is called:`, ch:[`Sublimation`,`Deposition`,`Freezing`,`Melting`], ans:1, exp:`Deposition is the reverse of sublimation: a gas turns directly into a solid without passing through the liquid state, such as frost forming from water vapor.` },
      { q:`Melting is the change of state from:`, ch:[`Liquid to gas`,`Solid to liquid`,`Gas to liquid`,`Liquid to solid`], ans:1, exp:`Melting occurs when a solid absorbs enough heat energy that its particles gain enough motion to flow past each other, becoming a liquid.` },
      { q:`Condensation is the change of state from:`, ch:[`Gas to liquid`,`Liquid to gas`,`Solid to liquid`,`Liquid to solid`], ans:0, exp:`During condensation, a gas loses energy (often by cooling), and its particles slow down and move closer together, forming a liquid.` },
      { q:`As a substance is heated and changes from solid to liquid to gas, the particles:`, ch:[`Move slower and get closer together`,`Gain energy and move faster/farther apart`,`Lose mass`,`Stop moving completely`], ans:1, exp:`Adding heat energy increases particle motion. As particles move faster, they overcome the attractive forces holding them close together, spreading farther apart.` },
      { q:`Freezing is the change of state from:`, ch:[`Gas to liquid`,`Solid to liquid`,`Liquid to solid`,`Liquid to gas`], ans:2, exp:`Freezing happens when a liquid loses enough heat energy that its particles slow down and lock into the fixed positions of a solid.` },
      { q:`At sea level, water boils at approximately:`, ch:[`0 degrees Celsius`,`50 degrees Celsius`,`100 degrees Celsius`,`212 degrees Celsius`], ans:2, exp:`At standard sea-level pressure, water boils at 100 degrees Celsius (which is equal to 212 degrees Fahrenheit).` },
      { q:`During a phase change (like melting ice), the temperature of the substance:`, ch:[`Increases steadily`,`Decreases steadily`,`Stays constant until the phase change is complete`,`Instantly jumps to the new state's temperature`], ans:2, exp:`During a phase change, added or removed energy goes into changing the arrangement of particles rather than their temperature, so temperature stays constant until the change is finished.` },
      { q:`Water freezes at what temperature (Celsius)?`, ch:[`100 degrees`,`32 degrees`,`0 degrees`,`-100 degrees`], ans:2, exp:`Pure water freezes at 0 degrees Celsius at standard atmospheric pressure (this is equal to 32 degrees Fahrenheit).` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp105`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Acids & Bases: The pH Scale`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`The pH scale typically ranges from:`, ch:[`0 to 7`,`0 to 14`,`1 to 10`,`-7 to 7`], ans:1, exp:`The common pH scale runs from 0 (most acidic) to 14 (most basic), with 7 being neutral.` },
      { q:`A substance with a pH of 7 is considered:`, ch:[`Acidic`,`Basic (alkaline)`,`Neutral`,`Radioactive`], ans:2, exp:`A pH of exactly 7, like pure water, is neutral, meaning it is neither acidic nor basic.` },
      { q:`A substance with a pH less than 7 is:`, ch:[`Basic`,`Neutral`,`Acidic`,`Undefined`], ans:2, exp:`Any pH value below 7 indicates an acidic substance; the lower the number, the more acidic it is.` },
      { q:`A substance with a pH greater than 7 is:`, ch:[`Acidic`,`Basic (alkaline)`,`Neutral`,`Explosive`], ans:1, exp:`Any pH value above 7 indicates a basic (alkaline) substance; the higher the number, the more basic it is.` },
      { q:`Which of these is an example of a strong acid?`, ch:[`Baking soda`,`Hydrochloric acid (HCl)`,`Sodium hydroxide`,`Pure water`], ans:1, exp:`Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid that releases many hydrogen ions (H+) when dissolved in water, giving it a low pH. The other options are basic or neutral.` },
      { q:`Which of these is an example of a base?`, ch:[`Lemon juice`,`Vinegar`,`Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)`,`Battery acid`], ans:2, exp:`Sodium hydroxide is a strong base that releases hydroxide ions (OH-) in water, giving it a high pH. Lemon juice, vinegar, and battery acid are all acidic.` },
      { q:`Acids taste:`, ch:[`Sweet`,`Sour`,`Bitter`,`Salty`], ans:1, exp:`Acids characteristically taste sour, like the citric acid in lemons or the acetic acid in vinegar. (Tasting unknown chemicals is never safe in real life.)` },
      { q:`Bases often feel:`, ch:[`Sour`,`Slippery`,`Gritty`,`Sticky like sugar`], ans:1, exp:`Many bases feel slippery or soapy to the touch, which is one reason soap (a base) feels slick on skin.` },
      { q:`Litmus paper turns red in the presence of:`, ch:[`A base`,`An acid`,`Pure water`,`A neutral substance`], ans:1, exp:`Blue litmus paper turns red when exposed to an acidic solution, making it a simple way to test for acids.` },
      { q:`Litmus paper turns blue in the presence of:`, ch:[`An acid`,`A base`,`A neutral salt`,`Water only`], ans:1, exp:`Red litmus paper turns blue when exposed to a basic (alkaline) solution.` },
      { q:`What ion do acids release when dissolved in water?`, ch:[`Hydroxide ion (OH-)`,`Hydrogen ion (H+)`,`Sodium ion (Na+)`,`Chloride ion only`], ans:1, exp:`Acids are defined by their ability to release hydrogen ions (H+) when dissolved in water, which is what makes a solution acidic.` },
      { q:`What ion do bases release when dissolved in water?`, ch:[`Hydrogen ion (H+)`,`Hydroxide ion (OH-)`,`Oxygen ion`,`Carbon ion`], ans:1, exp:`Bases release hydroxide ions (OH-) when dissolved in water, which is what makes a solution basic.` },
      { q:`Each whole number drop on the pH scale represents a change in acidity of:`, ch:[`2 times`,`10 times`,`100 times`,`No change, it's linear`], ans:1, exp:`The pH scale is logarithmic, so each single step down the scale represents a 10-fold increase in acidity (concentration of H+ ions).` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp106`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Mixtures & Solutions Basics`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`Salt water is an example of a:`, ch:[`Pure element`,`Compound`,`Mixture`,`Isotope`], ans:2, exp:`Salt water is a mixture because the salt and water are physically combined but not chemically bonded together, and the salt can be separated back out.` },
      { q:`In a solution, the substance that dissolves is called the:`, ch:[`Solvent`,`Solute`,`Suspension`,`Precipitate`], ans:1, exp:`The solute is the substance being dissolved, such as the salt in salt water. The solvent is the substance doing the dissolving, such as the water.` },
      { q:`In a solution, the substance that does the dissolving (usually present in larger amount) is called the:`, ch:[`Solute`,`Solvent`,`Colloid`,`Residue`], ans:1, exp:`The solvent is the substance present in the larger amount that dissolves the solute. Water is often called the "universal solvent" because it dissolves so many substances.` },
      { q:`Which of these is a homogeneous mixture (uniform throughout)?`, ch:[`Salad`,`Salt water`,`Sand and gravel`,`Oil and water shaken up`], ans:1, exp:`Salt water is homogeneous because the dissolved salt is evenly spread throughout the water, so every sample looks and tastes the same.` },
      { q:`Which of these is a heterogeneous mixture (not uniform throughout)?`, ch:[`Salt water`,`Air`,`Sand mixed with pebbles`,`Sugar water`], ans:2, exp:`Sand mixed with pebbles is heterogeneous because you can see distinct particles of different sizes that are not evenly or uniformly distributed.` },
      { q:`A mixture where solute particles are small enough that they never settle out is called:`, ch:[`A suspension`,`A solution`,`A compound`,`A colloid only`], ans:1, exp:`In a true solution, the solute particles are dissolved at the molecular or ionic level, so they stay evenly mixed and do not settle out over time.` },
      { q:`Increasing the temperature of water generally does what to the rate at which sugar dissolves?`, ch:[`Slows it down`,`Speeds it up`,`Has no effect`,`Stops dissolving completely`], ans:1, exp:`Warmer water molecules move faster and collide with the sugar more often and with more energy, which generally speeds up the dissolving process.` },
      { q:`Which method could you use to separate salt from salt water?`, ch:[`Filtration`,`Using a magnet`,`Evaporation`,`Sifting`], ans:2, exp:`Evaporating the water leaves the dissolved salt behind as a solid, since the salt does not evaporate at the same temperature as the water.` },
      { q:`Which method could you use to separate iron filings from sand?`, ch:[`Evaporation`,`Using a magnet`,`Distillation`,`Filtration`], ans:1, exp:`Iron is magnetic and sand is not, so a magnet can pull the iron filings out of the mixture, leaving the sand behind.` },
      { q:`A solution that has dissolved all the solute it can hold at a given temperature is called:`, ch:[`Unsaturated`,`Saturated`,`Diluted`,`Concentrated only`], ans:1, exp:`A saturated solution has reached the maximum amount of solute that can dissolve in the solvent at that temperature; any extra solute added will not dissolve.` },
      { q:`Which of the following best describes a "pure substance"?`, ch:[`Made of only one type of atom or molecule throughout`,`Any liquid`,`Any mixture of two solids`,`A mixture of gases`], ans:0, exp:`A pure substance, such as an element or a compound, contains only one type of particle throughout, unlike a mixture which contains two or more different substances.` },
      { q:`Filtration is a good method for separating:`, ch:[`Dissolved salt from water`,`Insoluble solid particles from a liquid`,`Two miscible liquids`,`Gases from each other`], ans:1, exp:`Filtration uses a filter with tiny holes to trap solid particles that are too large to pass through while letting the liquid flow past, so it works well for undissolved solids in a liquid.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp107`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Newton's Laws of Motion`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`Newton's First Law states that an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion, unless acted on by:`, ch:[`Gravity only`,`An unbalanced (net) force`,`Friction only`,`Its own mass`], ans:1, exp:`Newton's First Law says an object's motion will not change unless a net (unbalanced) outside force acts on it. This resistance to changes in motion is called inertia.` },
      { q:`Newton's First Law is also known as the law of:`, ch:[`Acceleration`,`Inertia`,`Action-reaction`,`Gravity`], ans:1, exp:`Newton's First Law is often called the law of inertia because it describes an object's natural tendency to resist changes in its state of motion.` },
      { q:`Newton's Second Law is expressed by the equation:`, ch:[`F = ma`,`E = mc^2`,`F = mv`,`a = F + m`], ans:0, exp:`Newton's Second Law states that force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma), relating the net force on an object to its mass and resulting acceleration.` },
      { q:`According to Newton's Second Law, if the net force on an object increases while mass stays constant, the acceleration will:`, ch:[`Decrease`,`Increase`,`Stay the same`,`Become zero`], ans:1, exp:`Since a = F/m, if force (F) increases while mass (m) stays the same, the acceleration (a) must also increase proportionally.` },
      { q:`According to Newton's Second Law, if mass increases while force stays constant, acceleration will:`, ch:[`Increase`,`Decrease`,`Stay the same`,`Reverse direction`], ans:1, exp:`Since a = F/m, increasing the mass while keeping force constant results in a smaller acceleration; more mass is harder to speed up with the same force.` },
      { q:`Newton's Third Law states that for every action there is:`, ch:[`No reaction`,`A smaller reaction`,`An equal and opposite reaction`,`A delayed reaction`], ans:2, exp:`Newton's Third Law states that forces come in pairs: when one object exerts a force on a second object, the second object exerts an equal force in the opposite direction back on the first.` },
      { q:`A rocket launches upward by pushing exhaust gases downward. This demonstrates:`, ch:[`Newton's First Law`,`Newton's Second Law`,`Newton's Third Law`,`The law of conservation of energy`], ans:2, exp:`The rocket pushes hot gases downward (action), and the gases push the rocket upward with equal force (reaction), which is a direct example of Newton's Third Law.` },
      { q:`Inertia is best described as:`, ch:[`The force of gravity on an object`,`An object's tendency to resist changes in its motion`,`The speed of an object`,`The weight of an object`], ans:1, exp:`Inertia is not a force; it is a property of matter describing how much an object resists a change in its velocity (starting, stopping, speeding up, or turning).` },
      { q:`An object with more mass has:`, ch:[`Less inertia`,`More inertia`,`No inertia`,`The same inertia as any other object`], ans:1, exp:`Inertia is directly related to mass: the more mass an object has, the more it resists changes to its motion, which is why heavier objects are harder to start or stop moving.` },
      { q:`If a 2 kg object experiences a net force of 10 N, what is its acceleration?`, ch:[`2 m/s^2`,`5 m/s^2`,`10 m/s^2`,`20 m/s^2`], ans:1, exp:`Using F = ma, a = F/m = 10 N / 2 kg = 5 m/s^2.` },
      { q:`A book resting on a table stays still because:`, ch:[`There are no forces acting on it`,`Gravity and the normal force from the table are balanced`,`It has no mass`,`Friction pushes it up`], ans:1, exp:`Gravity pulls the book down while the table pushes back up with an equal normal force, so the net force is zero and the book stays at rest, consistent with Newton's First Law.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is an example of Newton's First Law in action?`, ch:[`A car accelerating when the gas pedal is pressed`,`Passengers lurching forward when a car suddenly stops`,`A rocket pushing exhaust to move forward`,`A heavier object needing more force to accelerate the same amount`], ans:1, exp:`When a car stops suddenly, passengers keep moving forward due to inertia until a force (like a seatbelt) acts on them, which is a classic demonstration of Newton's First Law.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp108`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Speed, Velocity & Acceleration`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`Speed is defined as:`, ch:[`Distance traveled divided by time`,`Force divided by mass`,`Change in velocity divided by time`,`Mass times velocity`], ans:0, exp:`Speed measures how fast an object is moving, calculated as the distance traveled divided by the time it took, regardless of direction.` },
      { q:`The formula for average speed is:`, ch:[`v = d/t`,`v = t/d`,`v = d x t`,`v = m/d`], ans:0, exp:`Average speed equals total distance (d) divided by total time (t), written as v = d/t.` },
      { q:`Velocity differs from speed because velocity includes:`, ch:[`Units`,`Direction`,`Time`,`Mass`], ans:1, exp:`Velocity is speed with a specified direction (for example, 60 km/h north), making velocity a vector quantity while speed alone is not.` },
      { q:`Acceleration is defined as:`, ch:[`Distance divided by time`,`The rate of change of velocity over time`,`Speed multiplied by mass`,`Force divided by time`], ans:1, exp:`Acceleration describes how quickly an object's velocity changes over time, whether it is speeding up, slowing down, or changing direction.` },
      { q:`If a car speeds up, slows down, or changes direction, it is:`, ch:[`Moving at constant velocity`,`Accelerating`,`At rest`,`Experiencing no net force`], ans:1, exp:`Any change in speed or direction counts as acceleration, since acceleration is defined as any change in velocity over time.` },
      { q:`A car travels 150 km in 3 hours. What is its average speed?`, ch:[`30 km/h`,`50 km/h`,`450 km/h`,`3 km/h`], ans:1, exp:`Average speed = distance / time = 150 km / 3 h = 50 km/h.` },
      { q:`If a car's velocity changes from 0 m/s to 20 m/s in 4 seconds, what is its acceleration?`, ch:[`4 m/s^2`,`5 m/s^2`,`20 m/s^2`,`80 m/s^2`], ans:1, exp:`Acceleration = change in velocity / time = (20 m/s - 0 m/s) / 4 s = 5 m/s^2.` },
      { q:`Deceleration (slowing down) is an example of:`, ch:[`Zero acceleration`,`Positive speed only`,`Negative acceleration`,`Constant velocity`], ans:2, exp:`Deceleration is acceleration in the direction opposite to the object's motion, which is often described as negative acceleration because it reduces speed.` },
      { q:`Which of these units is correct for measuring acceleration?`, ch:[`m/s`,`m/s^2`,`m`,`s/m`], ans:1, exp:`Acceleration is the change in velocity (m/s) per unit time (s), so its units are m/s^2 (also written as m/s per s).` },
      { q:`An object moving in a circle at constant speed has:`, ch:[`Zero acceleration because speed is constant`,`Constant velocity`,`Acceleration, because its direction is constantly changing`,`No forces acting on it`], ans:2, exp:`Even though its speed does not change, an object moving in a circle is constantly changing direction, and since velocity includes direction, this counts as acceleration.` },
      { q:`On a distance-time graph, a straight diagonal line represents:`, ch:[`The object is at rest`,`The object is moving at constant speed`,`The object is accelerating`,`The object is moving backward`], ans:1, exp:`A straight diagonal line on a distance-time graph means equal distances are covered in equal time intervals, indicating constant speed.` },
      { q:`On a speed-time graph, a flat horizontal line represents:`, ch:[`Acceleration`,`Constant speed`,`The object is at rest`,`Deceleration`], ans:1, exp:`A flat horizontal line on a speed-time graph shows that speed is not changing over time, meaning the object is moving at a constant speed.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp109`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Energy: Kinetic & Potential`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`Kinetic energy is the energy of:`, ch:[`Position or stored energy`,`Motion`,`Heat only`,`Chemical bonds only`], ans:1, exp:`Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because it is moving. The faster an object moves, the more kinetic energy it has.` },
      { q:`The formula for kinetic energy is:`, ch:[`KE = mgh`,`KE = 1/2 m v^2`,`KE = mv`,`KE = ma`], ans:1, exp:`Kinetic energy equals one-half times mass times velocity squared: KE = 1/2 m v^2.` },
      { q:`Gravitational potential energy is given by the formula:`, ch:[`PE = 1/2 m v^2`,`PE = mgh`,`PE = mv`,`PE = m/gh`], ans:1, exp:`Gravitational potential energy equals mass times the acceleration due to gravity times height: PE = mgh.` },
      { q:`If an object's speed doubles, its kinetic energy:`, ch:[`Stays the same`,`Doubles`,`Quadruples (4x)`,`Halves`], ans:2, exp:`Because KE = 1/2 m v^2, kinetic energy depends on the square of velocity. Doubling velocity (2^2 = 4) quadruples the kinetic energy if mass stays the same.` },
      { q:`A ball held at the top of a hill has mostly:`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Potential energy`,`Sound energy`,`No energy`], ans:1, exp:`A stationary ball at height has gravitational potential energy stored due to its position, since it is not yet moving and has little to no kinetic energy.` },
      { q:`As the ball rolls down the hill, potential energy is converted into:`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Mass`,`Nuclear energy`,`Nothing, energy disappears`], ans:0, exp:`As height decreases and speed increases going down the hill, stored potential energy is converted into kinetic energy of motion.` },
      { q:`The Law of Conservation of Energy states that energy:`, ch:[`Can be created but not destroyed`,`Can be destroyed but not created`,`Cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed`,`Always increases over time`], ans:2, exp:`Energy cannot be created or destroyed; it can only change from one form to another, such as potential energy converting into kinetic energy.` },
      { q:`Which has more gravitational potential energy: a 10 kg box on a shelf 2 m high, or the same box on a shelf 4 m high?`, ch:[`The 2 m shelf`,`The 4 m shelf`,`They are equal`,`Cannot be determined`], ans:1, exp:`Since PE = mgh, and mass and gravity are the same in both cases, the box on the higher (4 m) shelf has greater potential energy.` },
      { q:`A roller coaster car at the very top of the first hill (before dropping) has:`, ch:[`Maximum kinetic energy`,`Maximum potential energy relative to the ride`,`Zero total energy`,`Only thermal energy`], ans:1, exp:`At the top of the first hill, the car is moving slowly (little kinetic energy) but is at its highest point, giving it maximum gravitational potential energy for the ride.` },
      { q:`Which of these is an example of stored (potential) energy?`, ch:[`A moving car`,`A stretched rubber band`,`A falling rock`,`Sound from a speaker`], ans:1, exp:`A stretched rubber band stores elastic potential energy due to its deformed shape, which is released as kinetic energy when it snaps back.` },
      { q:`Mechanical energy is best described as:`, ch:[`Only kinetic energy`,`Only potential energy`,`The sum of kinetic and potential energy`,`Energy from electricity`], ans:2, exp:`Mechanical energy is the total of an object's kinetic energy (motion) and potential energy (position), such as a pendulum swinging between both forms.` },
      { q:`If a 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s, what is its kinetic energy?`, ch:[`3 J`,`6 J`,`9 J`,`18 J`], ans:2, exp:`KE = 1/2 m v^2 = 1/2 (2 kg)(3 m/s)^2 = 1/2 (2)(9) = 9 J.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp110`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Work, Power & Simple Machines`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`In physics, work is done when:`, ch:[`A force causes an object to move a distance`,`You feel tired`,`A force is applied but nothing moves`,`Time passes`], ans:0, exp:`In physics, work is only done when a force causes an object to actually move some distance in the direction of the force. Pushing on something that does not move does no work, no matter how tiring it feels.` },
      { q:`The formula for work is:`, ch:[`W = F/d`,`W = F x d`,`W = m x a`,`W = F x t`], ans:1, exp:`Work equals force times the distance over which the force is applied, in the direction of motion: W = F x d.` },
      { q:`The unit of work (and energy) is the:`, ch:[`Newton`,`Watt`,`Joule`,`Pascal`], ans:2, exp:`Work and energy are both measured in joules (J). A newton measures force, a watt measures power, and a pascal measures pressure.` },
      { q:`Power is defined as:`, ch:[`The amount of work done`,`The rate at which work is done (work per unit time)`,`The force applied to an object`,`The distance an object travels`], ans:1, exp:`Power measures how quickly work is done or energy is transferred, calculated as work divided by the time it took.` },
      { q:`The formula for power is:`, ch:[`P = W/t`,`P = W x t`,`P = F x d`,`P = m x g`], ans:0, exp:`Power equals work divided by time: P = W/t. More power means the same amount of work is completed in less time.` },
      { q:`The unit of power is the:`, ch:[`Joule`,`Newton`,`Watt`,`Pascal`], ans:2, exp:`Power is measured in watts (W), where one watt equals one joule of work done per second.` },
      { q:`If you do 100 J of work in 5 seconds, your power output is:`, ch:[`20 W`,`500 W`,`0.05 W`,`105 W`], ans:0, exp:`Power = work / time = 100 J / 5 s = 20 W.` },
      { q:`A simple machine makes work easier primarily by:`, ch:[`Increasing the total amount of work needed`,`Reducing the amount of force needed to do the same work, often over a longer distance`,`Eliminating the need for work entirely`,`Increasing energy output`], ans:1, exp:`Simple machines trade off force and distance: they let you apply less force over a longer distance to accomplish the same amount of work, making tasks feel easier.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is a simple machine?`, ch:[`A car engine`,`A lever`,`A computer`,`A battery`], ans:1, exp:`A lever is one of the six classic simple machines, along with the wheel and axle, pulley, inclined plane, wedge, and screw.` },
      { q:`An inclined plane (ramp) makes lifting a heavy object easier by:`, ch:[`Decreasing the distance the object travels`,`Increasing the force required`,`Spreading the work over a longer distance so less force is needed`,`Eliminating gravity's effect`], ans:2, exp:`A ramp lets you push an object up a longer sloped path instead of lifting it straight up, requiring less force even though the object travels a greater distance.` },
      { q:`A pulley system is useful because it can:`, ch:[`Change the direction of force and/or reduce the force needed`,`Create energy out of nothing`,`Only work with electricity`,`Increase the total work done`], ans:0, exp:`Pulleys can redirect the force needed (for example, pulling down to lift something up) and, with multiple pulleys, can reduce the amount of force required to lift a load.` },
      { q:`Which of these is NOT one of the six classic simple machines?`, ch:[`Lever`,`Pulley`,`Wedge`,`Battery`], ans:3, exp:`A battery is a source of electrical energy, not a simple machine. The six classic simple machines are the lever, wheel and axle, pulley, inclined plane, wedge, and screw.` },
      { q:`If no distance is covered even though a force is applied (like pushing on a stationary wall), the amount of work done is:`, ch:[`Very large`,`Zero`,`Negative`,`Infinite`], ans:1, exp:`Since work requires movement (W = F x d), if the distance moved is zero, then the work done is also zero, no matter how much force is applied.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp111`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Electricity & Circuits Basics`, diff:`Medium`, qs:[
      { q:`Electric current is defined as:`, ch:[`The flow of electric charge`,`The resistance in a wire`,`The stored energy in a battery`,`The force between magnets`], ans:0, exp:`Electric current is the flow of electric charge (typically electrons) through a conductor, such as a wire.` },
      { q:`The unit of electric current is the:`, ch:[`Volt`,`Ohm`,`Ampere (amp)`,`Watt`], ans:2, exp:`Electric current is measured in amperes, commonly called amps, which describe the rate of flow of electric charge.` },
      { q:`The unit of voltage (electric potential difference) is the:`, ch:[`Ampere`,`Volt`,`Ohm`,`Joule`], ans:1, exp:`Voltage, or electric potential difference, is measured in volts, which represent the energy per unit of charge that pushes current through a circuit.` },
      { q:`The unit of electrical resistance is the:`, ch:[`Volt`,`Amp`,`Ohm`,`Watt`], ans:2, exp:`Electrical resistance, which measures how much a material opposes the flow of current, is measured in ohms.` },
      { q:`Ohm's Law is expressed as:`, ch:[`V = I x R`,`V = I/R`,`V = I + R`,`I = V x R`], ans:0, exp:`Ohm's Law states that voltage equals current times resistance: V = I x R.` },
      { q:`In a series circuit, if one bulb burns out:`, ch:[`The rest of the circuit still works`,`The entire circuit stops working because the path is broken`,`The other bulbs get brighter`,`Nothing changes`], ans:1, exp:`A series circuit has only one path for current to flow. If that path is broken by a burnt-out bulb, current cannot flow anywhere else in the circuit, so everything stops working.` },
      { q:`In a parallel circuit, if one branch is broken:`, ch:[`The whole circuit stops working`,`The other branches can still work because they have separate paths`,`All bulbs immediately go out`,`The voltage becomes zero everywhere`], ans:1, exp:`A parallel circuit provides multiple separate paths for current, so if one branch is broken, current can still flow through the other branches, keeping them working.` },
      { q:`Materials that allow electric current to flow easily, like copper, are called:`, ch:[`Insulators`,`Conductors`,`Resistors`,`Capacitors`], ans:1, exp:`Conductors, such as copper and other metals, have electrons that move freely, allowing electric current to pass through them easily.` },
      { q:`Materials that do not allow electric current to flow easily, like rubber, are called:`, ch:[`Conductors`,`Insulators`,`Semiconductors only`,`Superconductors`], ans:1, exp:`Insulators, such as rubber and plastic, strongly resist the flow of electric current, which is why they are used to coat wires for safety.` },
      { q:`Using Ohm's Law, if voltage is 12 V and resistance is 4 ohms, what is the current?`, ch:[`3 A`,`48 A`,`8 A`,`16 A`], ans:0, exp:`From V = I x R, I = V/R = 12 V / 4 ohms = 3 A.` },
      { q:`A circuit must form a complete, unbroken loop for current to flow. This loop is called:`, ch:[`An open circuit`,`A closed circuit`,`A short circuit`,`A broken circuit`], ans:1, exp:`A closed circuit provides an unbroken path from the power source, through the components, and back again, allowing current to flow continuously.` },
      { q:`A battery provides the energy in a circuit by creating a difference in:`, ch:[`Resistance`,`Electric potential (voltage)`,`Current only`,`Wire thickness`], ans:1, exp:`A battery creates a voltage difference (electric potential difference) between its two terminals, which pushes current through the connected circuit.` },
      { q:`Static electricity is caused by:`, ch:[`A build-up of electric charge on the surface of an object`,`Current flowing continuously through a wire`,`Magnetic fields only`,`Heat energy alone`], ans:0, exp:`Static electricity results from an imbalance of charge, often from electrons transferring between surfaces through friction, building up on an object until it discharges.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp112`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Waves, Sound & Light Basics`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`A wave is best described as a disturbance that transfers:`, ch:[`Matter from one place to another`,`Energy from one place to another without transferring matter`,`Only light`,`Only sound`], ans:1, exp:`Waves carry energy from one location to another. The medium itself (like water molecules or air particles) mostly stays in place, oscillating rather than traveling with the wave.` },
      { q:`The distance between two consecutive crests of a wave is called the:`, ch:[`Amplitude`,`Frequency`,`Wavelength`,`Period`], ans:2, exp:`Wavelength is the distance from one point on a wave (like a crest) to the same point on the next wave cycle (the next crest).` },
      { q:`The height of a wave from its resting position is called the:`, ch:[`Wavelength`,`Amplitude`,`Frequency`,`Speed`], ans:1, exp:`Amplitude measures the maximum displacement of a wave from its rest (equilibrium) position, and it relates to how much energy the wave carries.` },
      { q:`Frequency is measured in units called:`, ch:[`Meters`,`Seconds`,`Hertz`,`Newtons`], ans:2, exp:`Frequency, the number of wave cycles that pass a point per second, is measured in hertz (Hz).` },
      { q:`Sound waves need a medium (like air or water) to travel through because they are:`, ch:[`Electromagnetic waves`,`Mechanical waves`,`Light waves`,`Not real waves`], ans:1, exp:`Sound is a mechanical wave, meaning it travels by vibrating particles of a physical medium, so it cannot travel where there is no matter.` },
      { q:`Sound cannot travel through:`, ch:[`Air`,`Water`,`Steel`,`A vacuum (empty space)`], ans:3, exp:`Because sound needs particles to vibrate and transmit the wave, it cannot travel through a vacuum, which has no matter present.` },
      { q:`Light waves are a type of:`, ch:[`Mechanical wave`,`Electromagnetic wave`,`Sound wave`,`Longitudinal wave only`], ans:1, exp:`Light is an electromagnetic wave, made of oscillating electric and magnetic fields, which does not require a medium to travel.` },
      { q:`Unlike sound waves, light waves can travel through:`, ch:[`Only solids`,`Only liquids`,`A vacuum (empty space)`,`Nothing at all`], ans:2, exp:`Because light is an electromagnetic wave and not a mechanical wave, it does not need a medium and can travel through the vacuum of space, which is how sunlight reaches Earth.` },
      { q:`In general, how does sound travel fastest?`, ch:[`Through a vacuum`,`Through air`,`Through solids`,`It travels the same speed in everything`], ans:2, exp:`Sound generally travels fastest through solids, where particles are closely packed and can transmit vibrations quickly, slower through liquids, and slowest through gases like air.` },
      { q:`When white light passes through a prism, it separates into a rainbow of colors because:`, ch:[`Different colors have different wavelengths and bend by different amounts`,`The prism adds color to the light`,`White light contains no color`,`The prism absorbs all light`], ans:0, exp:`White light is made of many colors of different wavelengths. Each wavelength bends (refracts) by a slightly different amount passing through the prism, spreading the colors apart.` },
      { q:`The bending of light as it passes from one medium into another (like from air into water) is called:`, ch:[`Reflection`,`Refraction`,`Diffraction`,`Absorption`], ans:1, exp:`Refraction is the bending of a wave, such as light, as it passes from one medium into another where its speed changes.` },
      { q:`The bouncing of light off a surface, like a mirror, is called:`, ch:[`Refraction`,`Reflection`,`Diffusion`,`Transmission`], ans:1, exp:`Reflection occurs when light waves bounce off a surface rather than passing through or being absorbed by it, which is how mirrors form images.` },
      { q:`A higher frequency sound wave is perceived by the human ear as:`, ch:[`Louder`,`Quieter`,`A higher pitch`,`A lower pitch`], ans:2, exp:`Frequency of a sound wave determines pitch: higher frequency sound waves are heard as higher-pitched sounds, while lower frequencies are heard as lower-pitched sounds.` },
    ]
  },
  {
    id:`cp113`, cat:`Physical Sciences`, title:`Earth Science: Weather, Climate & the Water Cycle`, diff:`Easy`, qs:[
      { q:`Weather refers to:`, ch:[`The average conditions over many years in a region`,`The short-term conditions of the atmosphere at a specific place and time`,`Only temperature`,`Only precipitation`], ans:1, exp:`Weather describes the atmospheric conditions, such as temperature, precipitation, and wind, at a particular place over a short period of time, like a day.` },
      { q:`Climate refers to:`, ch:[`The condition of the atmosphere on a particular day`,`The long-term, average weather patterns of a region over many years`,`A single storm event`,`Only wind speed`], ans:1, exp:`Climate describes the typical, long-term weather patterns of a region, usually measured as averages over 30 years or more.` },
      { q:`The process by which water changes from a liquid to a gas (water vapor), often by heat from the sun, is called:`, ch:[`Condensation`,`Evaporation`,`Precipitation`,`Collection`], ans:1, exp:`Evaporation occurs when liquid water absorbs enough heat energy, usually from the sun, that it changes into water vapor and rises into the atmosphere.` },
      { q:`The process by which water vapor cools and changes back into liquid water droplets, forming clouds, is called:`, ch:[`Evaporation`,`Precipitation`,`Condensation`,`Runoff`], ans:2, exp:`Condensation occurs when rising water vapor cools in the atmosphere and changes back into tiny liquid water droplets, which cluster together to form clouds.` },
      { q:`Water falling from clouds as rain, snow, sleet, or hail is called:`, ch:[`Evaporation`,`Condensation`,`Precipitation`,`Transpiration`], ans:2, exp:`Precipitation is any form of water, liquid or solid, that falls from clouds to Earth's surface, including rain, snow, sleet, and hail.` },
      { q:`The movement of water over the land surface back to rivers, lakes, and oceans is called:`, ch:[`Runoff`,`Sublimation`,`Condensation`,`Evaporation`], ans:0, exp:`Runoff is precipitation that flows over the land surface, eventually draining into streams, rivers, lakes, and oceans as part of the water cycle.` },
      { q:`The release of water vapor from plants (through their leaves) is called:`, ch:[`Precipitation`,`Transpiration`,`Condensation`,`Runoff`], ans:1, exp:`Transpiration is the process by which plants absorb water through their roots and release water vapor into the atmosphere through tiny openings in their leaves.` },
      { q:`Which layer of Earth's atmosphere is closest to the surface and where most weather occurs?`, ch:[`Stratosphere`,`Mesosphere`,`Troposphere`,`Thermosphere`], ans:2, exp:`The troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere, extending from Earth's surface up several miles, and it is where nearly all weather occurs.` },
      { q:`Clouds are formed primarily through the process of:`, ch:[`Evaporation`,`Condensation of water vapor around tiny particles`,`Precipitation`,`Runoff`], ans:1, exp:`Clouds form when water vapor condenses into tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals around small particles like dust in the atmosphere.` },
      { q:`The main source of energy that drives Earth's water cycle and weather is:`, ch:[`The moon`,`The sun`,`The Earth's core`,`Wind alone`], ans:1, exp:`The sun's energy heats water, causing evaporation, and unevenly heats Earth's surface and atmosphere, driving the winds and weather patterns that power the water cycle.` },
      { q:`Warm air compared to cold air is generally:`, ch:[`Denser and sinks`,`Less dense and rises`,`Always still`,`Heavier`], ans:1, exp:`Warm air is less dense than cold air, so it tends to rise, while denser cold air tends to sink, driving much of the movement seen in weather patterns.` },
      { q:`A front in weather refers to:`, ch:[`A type of cloud`,`The boundary between two air masses with different temperatures/densities`,`A measurement of humidity`,`A wind speed category`], ans:1, exp:`A weather front is the boundary where two air masses of different temperature and density meet, often bringing changes in weather like precipitation or wind shifts.` },
      { q:`Global climate patterns are influenced by all of the following EXCEPT:`, ch:[`Ocean currents`,`The sun's energy`,`The phase of the moon`,`Atmospheric circulation`], ans:2, exp:`Ocean currents, the sun's energy, and atmospheric circulation all significantly shape long-term climate patterns, but the phase of the moon has no meaningful effect on Earth's climate.` },
    ]
  },
];
